1
00:00:01,823 --> 00:00:04,906
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I was thinking about the Metta Sutta.

2
00:00:08,790 --> 00:00:11,993
[SPEAKER_00]: And, just going through that, there's quite a lot in it, actually.

3
00:00:12,153 --> 00:00:16,877
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we can stop wherever feels right.

4
00:00:20,781 --> 00:00:22,523
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'll read it verse by verse.

5
00:00:26,784 --> 00:00:34,210
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it begins with what should be done by one's skillful in good, so as to gain the state of peace?

6
00:00:38,893 --> 00:00:40,014
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of a question, really.

7
00:00:42,436 --> 00:00:44,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what we spoke before.

8
00:00:47,459 --> 00:00:52,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Only the person with the right view knows good as good and bad as bad.

9
00:00:54,445 --> 00:00:56,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Knows kusala is kusala and akusala is akusala.

10
00:00:59,749 --> 00:01:03,452
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, that also it applies to somebody in the right view.

11
00:01:05,873 --> 00:01:11,377
[SPEAKER_01]: What should be done once you know kusala is kusala and akusala is akusala?

12
00:01:11,717 --> 00:01:12,978
[SPEAKER_00]: One who is skillful in good.

13
00:01:12,998 --> 00:01:13,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

14
00:01:14,079 --> 00:01:15,961
[SPEAKER_01]: One who knows good is good, bad is bad.

15
00:01:16,441 --> 00:01:19,943
[SPEAKER_00]: What should he do to have his mind in a peaceful state?

16
00:01:23,278 --> 00:01:26,760
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, let him be able, upright and straightforward.

17
00:01:29,782 --> 00:01:32,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Isn't he already like that?

18
00:01:32,383 --> 00:01:34,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there's always room for improvement.

19
00:01:35,045 --> 00:01:40,208
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the paññā is upright and straightforward from Puthujjana's point of view, but not from an Arahant's point of view.

20
00:01:40,428 --> 00:01:48,793
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, it is easy to speak to, gentle and not proud.

21
00:01:51,452 --> 00:01:53,453
[SPEAKER_00]: And this gentle got me.

22
00:01:54,654 --> 00:01:57,035
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not... he's not sensitive.

23
00:01:58,195 --> 00:01:58,435
No.

24
00:02:00,016 --> 00:02:00,336
[SPEAKER_00]: You know.

25
00:02:01,217 --> 00:02:02,297
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's not what gentle is.

26
00:02:03,478 --> 00:02:06,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you're non-gentle when you have intentions of harm.

27
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:13,602
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're unable to have... to act out of intentions of harm, you're gentle.

28
00:02:14,603 --> 00:02:18,985
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean you will necessarily come across as kind and gentle.

29
00:02:20,106 --> 00:02:20,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Because...

30
00:02:21,602 --> 00:02:24,022
[SPEAKER_01]: People don't have criteria for what truly kind and gentle is.

31
00:02:27,203 --> 00:02:27,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Or proud.

32
00:02:28,683 --> 00:02:29,404
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

33
00:02:29,604 --> 00:02:30,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Not proud.

34
00:02:30,344 --> 00:02:32,504
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot be rooted in your vanity, in your conceit.

35
00:02:35,425 --> 00:02:36,325
[SPEAKER_00]: He's contented.

36
00:02:38,625 --> 00:02:40,146
[SPEAKER_00]: Easily, easy to support.

37
00:02:40,986 --> 00:02:42,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Easily satisfied, yeah.

38
00:02:43,406 --> 00:02:46,367
[SPEAKER_00]: With few tasks and living very lightly.

39
00:02:48,025 --> 00:02:53,148
[SPEAKER_00]: These faculties are serene, sure, are serene, prudent and modest.

40
00:02:53,528 --> 00:02:55,690
[SPEAKER_00]: This is interesting.

41
00:02:56,010 --> 00:02:58,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Unswayed by the emotions of the clans.

42
00:02:59,692 --> 00:03:04,055
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unswayed by the emotions of others.

43
00:03:04,375 --> 00:03:05,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Of others, yeah.

44
00:03:05,536 --> 00:03:06,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Of other authorities.

45
00:03:07,397 --> 00:03:09,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Of other traditions, of other establishments.

46
00:03:11,019 --> 00:03:16,463
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, when you say, oh, the rules of a clan means if you belong to it, you must obey by that.

47
00:03:17,455 --> 00:03:25,623
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like belonging to a greater, eh... identity, which is usually everybody does... belongs to some greater identity to some extent.

48
00:03:26,784 --> 00:03:31,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Be it culture, religion, education or something makes you belong to the... to that group identity.

49
00:03:33,811 --> 00:03:36,033
[SPEAKER_01]: But that actually requires a wrong view.

50
00:03:37,912 --> 00:03:39,993
[SPEAKER_01]: It's rooted in the wrong view, that kind of belonging.

51
00:03:40,353 --> 00:03:43,434
[SPEAKER_01]: It's rooted in you not being transparent in regard to your own individuality.

52
00:03:43,954 --> 00:03:56,117
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the one who has the right view, who has developed his mind, he cannot, then, eh... ignore the fact that he always remains enclosed within himself.

53
00:03:58,835 --> 00:04:07,020
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, if he chooses to follow what clan says you should do, he'll only do so because he knows good is good and bad is bad, not because the clan said so.

54
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:07,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

55
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:13,123
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't know for yourself, you will be going by the words of others, by the authority of others, by the...

56
00:04:13,163 --> 00:04:13,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Majority.

57
00:04:13,704 --> 00:04:15,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Majority of others, exactly.

58
00:04:15,065 --> 00:04:21,468
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another way of... Oh, well, look, I don't understand this, I don't understand that, but this is more numerous.

59
00:04:22,329 --> 00:04:24,270
[SPEAKER_01]: There's more people saying this than that.

60
00:04:25,030 --> 00:04:27,352
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll just go with the majority on this one.

61
00:04:28,350 --> 00:04:30,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, either way, you don't understand for yourself.

62
00:04:30,030 --> 00:04:34,051
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even, say, if that majority were right, you are in the wrong.

63
00:04:34,611 --> 00:04:37,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not understanding for yourself, you're going just by the majority of it.

64
00:04:38,452 --> 00:04:43,133
[SPEAKER_00]: You're being swayed by... you've got no grounding yourself.

65
00:04:43,153 --> 00:04:43,873
[SPEAKER_00]: No grounding, yeah.

66
00:04:43,893 --> 00:04:45,854
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just circumstantial.

67
00:04:46,174 --> 00:04:55,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Circumstantially occurred that, in this instance, majority was right and... but you, as a rule of thumb, go with the majority out of your own weakness and, basically, lack of understanding.

68
00:04:56,436 --> 00:05:13,528
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, literally, like for Sotāpanna, when he develops the right view, when he knows kusala is kusala, kusala is kusala, is the sutta, say, even if the whole world would say the Buddha was wrong, or that's wrong, if he knows that as right, he would go with what he knows is right.

69
00:05:14,129 --> 00:05:18,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, from Bhutujana's point of view, that sounds like an extremely arrogant and proud position.

70
00:05:19,113 --> 00:05:20,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Who do you think you are?

71
00:05:20,273 --> 00:05:22,275
[SPEAKER_01]: You know better than the entire world.

72
00:05:24,375 --> 00:05:27,138
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it's factually, he knows better than the entire world.

73
00:05:27,478 --> 00:05:28,419
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Sotāpanna is.

74
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:33,385
[SPEAKER_00]: But he doesn't just think he knows better than the entire world in every single case or something.

75
00:05:33,465 --> 00:05:33,865
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

76
00:05:34,025 --> 00:05:38,730
[SPEAKER_01]: His thoughts are not rooted in I am better than the entire world, I am worse, or I am equal.

77
00:05:39,291 --> 00:05:45,338
[SPEAKER_01]: His thoughts are rooted in recognizing kusala is a kusala, kusala is kusala, a kusala is a kusala.

78
00:05:46,332 --> 00:05:48,813
[SPEAKER_01]: So, wholesome is wholesome, unwholesome is unwholesome.

79
00:05:49,433 --> 00:05:55,694
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the whole world says this is unwholesome, but he knows that to be wholesome, he will not go with the entire world.

80
00:05:58,155 --> 00:06:05,636
[SPEAKER_01]: If the whole world says, yes, this is wholesome, and he knows that it's wholesome, he'll go with it, because it's wholesome, not because the entire world said so.

81
00:06:06,997 --> 00:06:12,298
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how he will not be swayed by what clans say to do, because he has the internal criteria.

82
00:06:13,628 --> 00:06:34,362
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will never develop that internal criteria if you never basically look beyond and make effort to understand things for yourself on that individual basis, but instead always seek shelter in the safety of numbers, crowd, traditions, authority, which is the whole Kalama Sutta.

83
00:06:34,662 --> 00:06:39,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Do not go by the majority for its own sake, by the tradition.

84
00:06:40,326 --> 00:06:41,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Find out for yourself.

85
00:06:42,843 --> 00:06:44,684
[SPEAKER_01]: And many people kind of think they understand that.

86
00:06:44,764 --> 00:06:47,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes, of course, find out for myself, experience for myself.

87
00:06:47,727 --> 00:06:48,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Personal truth.

88
00:06:48,447 --> 00:06:53,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, when it comes to drilling their views down, it's going to be, well, most people say this.

89
00:06:53,931 --> 00:06:57,794
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I'm right, because I just subscribe to whatever else people say.

90
00:06:58,915 --> 00:07:02,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but do you understand that as right, because it's right?

91
00:07:03,238 --> 00:07:06,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Or do you just basically seek the safety in the group?

92
00:07:10,724 --> 00:07:13,305
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you do, you'll never develop the right view.

93
00:07:15,186 --> 00:07:17,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Or have a peaceful mind.

94
00:07:17,108 --> 00:07:19,589
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not have a peaceful mind without the right view.

95
00:07:20,609 --> 00:07:22,230
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no peace without understanding.

96
00:07:22,731 --> 00:07:24,312
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no understanding without the right view.

97
00:07:27,353 --> 00:07:29,074
[SPEAKER_01]: There is safety in the numbers, by the way.

98
00:07:29,554 --> 00:07:34,517
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that the safety you get from the group or majority or belonging to a tradition or a group identity.

99
00:07:35,378 --> 00:07:36,898
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that it's not safe.

100
00:07:37,099 --> 00:07:37,979
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that it's not real.

101
00:07:38,319 --> 00:07:38,599
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

102
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:39,580
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's flawed.

103
00:07:41,211 --> 00:07:42,351
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not a true safety.

104
00:07:45,372 --> 00:07:47,273
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the safety that you seek.

105
00:07:49,174 --> 00:07:52,955
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's only something that's sort of closest to safety that you know.

106
00:07:53,175 --> 00:07:58,957
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you look at it properly, investigate it, you realize actually it's not a safety.

107
00:07:58,977 --> 00:07:59,857
[SPEAKER_01]: It's safety of ignorance.

108
00:08:00,968 --> 00:08:03,449
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter what the size of the group is.

109
00:08:03,969 --> 00:08:07,470
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact is, I don't know for myself that what they say is right.

110
00:08:07,910 --> 00:08:13,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's on the level of my choosing to believe that they are right, because it makes me feel comfortable.

111
00:08:15,293 --> 00:08:17,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if they happen to be, why...

112
00:08:17,114 --> 00:08:17,934
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all secondary.

113
00:08:18,014 --> 00:08:18,834
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all secondary, yeah.

114
00:08:18,974 --> 00:08:19,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

115
00:08:20,375 --> 00:08:22,635
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you can find the right group, the group of arahants.

116
00:08:23,596 --> 00:08:29,118
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you seek the shelter of the... if you seek the safety, basically, and

117
00:08:30,566 --> 00:08:35,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Follow what they say, just so you'll be safe, not because you understood it, you won't be safe.

118
00:08:36,250 --> 00:08:39,472
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can live with the Buddha himself, and you'll still be a patujana.

119
00:08:40,893 --> 00:08:55,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not allowing it, basically, to penetrate on the levels it should, which is on that individual basis of recognition of fundamental not-safety, non-safety, fundamental exposure of existence, fundamental impermanence, fear, and all of that.

120
00:08:57,944 --> 00:08:59,445
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's always why people go with that.

121
00:09:00,317 --> 00:09:28,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you know, often we... you saw that, like, you know, living in the various monasteries and stuff, people would... would sometimes follow or come, having been following certain teachings that they know themselves were not adequate, because they've been doing for 20 years and still haven't overcome things that even... they made them start practicing, but they couldn't give it up, because there's nothing else left, which means they would have to then accept their individual responsibility for that exposure and not knowing.

122
00:09:29,322 --> 00:09:36,263
[SPEAKER_01]: So, although they know the teachings they've been following were not adequate, they're still too afraid to admit that to themselves.

123
00:09:37,263 --> 00:09:41,804
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's all like, oh, it didn't work for me, but I mean, have you understood it or no?

124
00:09:42,144 --> 00:09:43,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Have you... you made the effort to understand.

125
00:09:43,945 --> 00:09:49,626
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what did you... to what extent did you... you must have some understanding, if you recognize that it doesn't work for you.

126
00:09:50,206 --> 00:09:51,246
[SPEAKER_01]: What is that understanding?

127
00:09:52,126 --> 00:09:54,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Why it doesn't work for you?

128
00:09:54,466 --> 00:09:55,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it because it's inadequate?

129
00:09:55,627 --> 00:09:57,807
[SPEAKER_01]: So, already have a sense of, oh, this is inadequate.

130
00:09:58,762 --> 00:10:02,503
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're still afraid to take responsibility for that own recognition.

131
00:10:03,443 --> 00:10:15,025
[SPEAKER_01]: And instead, I'll just pretend and ignore the inadequacy, so I can still partake in the safety of numbers, safety of tradition, safety of an established norm, and so on.

132
00:10:17,086 --> 00:10:18,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Safety of the meditation method.

133
00:10:20,886 --> 00:10:21,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, comfort zone.

134
00:10:21,827 --> 00:10:22,627
[SPEAKER_00]: I know what I'm doing.

135
00:10:22,847 --> 00:10:23,687
[SPEAKER_00]: I know how to do this.

136
00:10:25,008 --> 00:10:28,750
[SPEAKER_01]: I kind of, in the back of my mind, know that it hasn't resulted in things I was hoping it would.

137
00:10:28,830 --> 00:10:30,951
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't result in things that the sutta says it should result.

138
00:10:31,491 --> 00:10:39,836
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yes, it's less uncomfortable than if I go against it and do away with it.

139
00:10:42,077 --> 00:10:45,638
[SPEAKER_01]: I feel less exposed if you pretend there is no problem.

140
00:10:49,380 --> 00:10:52,002
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unswayed by the emotions of the plans,

141
00:10:53,727 --> 00:10:59,491
[SPEAKER_00]: And let him never do the slightest thing that other wise men might hold blameable.

142
00:10:59,611 --> 00:10:59,832
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

143
00:11:00,512 --> 00:11:01,213
[SPEAKER_01]: The wise men.

144
00:11:01,673 --> 00:11:03,695
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the other men with right view would hold blameable.

145
00:11:03,855 --> 00:11:04,695
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is blameable?

146
00:11:05,296 --> 00:11:06,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Wrong view is the most blameworthy thing.

147
00:11:07,958 --> 00:11:12,701
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let him not entertain wrong view in any extent, in regard to anything, even for a second.

148
00:11:13,002 --> 00:11:14,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the most blameworthy thing.

149
00:11:15,063 --> 00:11:15,584
[SPEAKER_00]: But other...

150
00:11:16,024 --> 00:11:19,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Killing, stealing, cheating, lying, all wise men agree that is

151
00:11:21,122 --> 00:11:21,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Unwholesome.

152
00:11:21,903 --> 00:11:23,544
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know for yourself it's unwholesome.

153
00:11:26,086 --> 00:11:32,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you might not necessarily, at that stage, you might not necessarily see sensuality as directly unwholesome.

154
00:11:34,392 --> 00:11:42,157
[SPEAKER_01]: But by the extension of the principles you have understood, by the extension of that wisdom, you can safely assume that it's most likely unwholesome.

155
00:11:43,158 --> 00:11:45,360
[SPEAKER_01]: And then act accordingly.

156
00:11:45,420 --> 00:11:46,220
[SPEAKER_01]: And restrain yourself.

157
00:11:46,440 --> 00:11:48,362
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you will come to realize that it was unwholesome.

158
00:11:54,201 --> 00:12:02,607
[SPEAKER_00]: But also, he's not swayed by the emotion of the clan, but he listens to... Oh, yeah, exactly, because he's not conceited.

159
00:12:03,908 --> 00:12:08,912
[SPEAKER_01]: He does not dismiss the clans because he is self-centered and proud.

160
00:12:09,992 --> 00:12:21,581
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not swayed by their emotions, doesn't mean he has no perspective on their emotion, which equally then means he will also have perspective on that which is factual wisdom.

161
00:12:22,720 --> 00:12:35,904
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if somebody does not belong to the clan he approves of, or he was born with, or brought up with, he would recognize when somebody says something that is correct, that is along the principle of kusala is kusala, akusala is akusala.

162
00:12:36,525 --> 00:12:38,485
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he would recognize wisdom in others.

163
00:12:44,527 --> 00:12:45,107
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's what I mean.

164
00:12:45,147 --> 00:12:52,470
[SPEAKER_01]: From from a point of view of a patujana without a right view, that sounds like the most conceited thing.

165
00:12:55,202 --> 00:12:56,463
[SPEAKER_01]: All the teachers say this.

166
00:12:57,143 --> 00:12:58,683
[SPEAKER_01]: All the Buddhist traditions say this.

167
00:12:59,103 --> 00:13:00,904
[SPEAKER_01]: And who do you think you are saying the opposite?

168
00:13:01,464 --> 00:13:07,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, sure, it is possible that one who is saying the opposite might be doing simply because he's so conceited.

169
00:13:08,406 --> 00:13:10,186
[SPEAKER_01]: So he just wants to go against everybody else.

170
00:13:11,487 --> 00:13:14,507
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you listen what else that person has to say.

171
00:13:14,667 --> 00:13:15,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it just a mad rant?

172
00:13:16,568 --> 00:13:19,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are they making sense in other regards as well?

173
00:13:19,229 --> 00:13:20,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you realize, oh, well, they are.

174
00:13:21,600 --> 00:13:27,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, even if the whole world says one thing, there is something to this, so maybe it's worth investigating.

175
00:13:28,424 --> 00:13:34,327
[SPEAKER_01]: It's certainly not worth dismissing it, just because majority does not agree with it.

176
00:13:35,628 --> 00:13:40,030
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, fundamentally, there is only one Buddha in the world, when it arises.

177
00:13:40,770 --> 00:13:41,711
[SPEAKER_01]: There are no two Buddhas.

178
00:13:41,791 --> 00:13:43,592
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible to be at the same time.

179
00:13:44,833 --> 00:13:47,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, the Buddha is as alone as he gets.

180
00:13:51,418 --> 00:13:59,364
[SPEAKER_01]: He's the only, the first soul being that's against the grain of the entire universe, not just this world.

181
00:14:03,567 --> 00:14:09,592
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, when that man met him, straight after his enlightenment, and said, well, you look so calm, who's teaching did you follow?

182
00:14:09,752 --> 00:14:14,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Because this man assumed you must follow someone, some tradition, some observant, some tradition.

183
00:14:15,416 --> 00:14:18,038
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha factually said, I follow no one's teaching.

184
00:14:18,198 --> 00:14:19,019
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm my own guide.

185
00:14:19,119 --> 00:14:20,540
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm the supreme leader of men.

186
00:14:21,942 --> 00:14:23,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Pure fact.

187
00:14:24,464 --> 00:14:25,526
[SPEAKER_01]: But how did this man take it?

188
00:14:26,146 --> 00:14:27,167
[SPEAKER_01]: All right, so be it.

189
00:14:27,387 --> 00:14:27,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Good for you.

190
00:14:29,469 --> 00:14:29,970
[SPEAKER_01]: And walked off.

191
00:14:31,712 --> 00:14:35,896
[SPEAKER_01]: He did not believe him and he thought, look at this conceited fool.

192
00:14:35,916 --> 00:14:41,581
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what I mean.

193
00:14:41,621 --> 00:14:43,043
[SPEAKER_01]: That should not be your criteria.

194
00:14:43,936 --> 00:14:55,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he might have been factually, say, a person who declares that, might be factually conceited, or just sort of being argumentative, or just going against whatever, like steering things up.

195
00:14:56,603 --> 00:15:00,045
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you listen what else they have to say, and then you'll see.

196
00:15:00,365 --> 00:15:01,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, wait, there is something more to this.

197
00:15:03,387 --> 00:15:04,707
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that they're just negating.

198
00:15:05,107 --> 00:15:06,908
[SPEAKER_01]: They're providing reasons for the negating.

199
00:15:07,449 --> 00:15:09,430
[SPEAKER_01]: They're providing rational explanations.

200
00:15:09,950 --> 00:15:13,232
[SPEAKER_01]: They tie in with other things that, you know, could be interpreted that way.

201
00:15:16,673 --> 00:15:20,436
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that's the case, then you should investigate it further, and then use that as a criteria.

202
00:15:20,456 --> 00:15:27,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because, oh, a hundred people say one thing, and one person says the other.

203
00:15:27,502 --> 00:15:28,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, he's wrong.

204
00:15:29,864 --> 00:15:41,554
[SPEAKER_01]: And sometimes people even know that person in minority is not wrong, but they still try to argue a point that you should still go with the majority, and not disturb the established order of majority.

205
00:15:42,877 --> 00:15:52,161
[SPEAKER_01]: That is true only when the... if... when non-disturbing of the established order of the clans, of the tradition, would not go against the message you have.

206
00:15:53,822 --> 00:16:02,266
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you can go with the order, without compromising the message that you have, which is against what the order's message is, then it's fine.

207
00:16:02,306 --> 00:16:03,207
[SPEAKER_01]: You can go with the order.

208
00:16:04,227 --> 00:16:12,451
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you can... if going with the order means compromising the message that you have, that is correct, that the whole order disagrees with, then you shouldn't go with the order.

209
00:16:13,218 --> 00:16:17,559
[SPEAKER_01]: You should stick with the message, because, well, that's the only thing that matters.

210
00:16:17,739 --> 00:16:18,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Right view or wrong view.

211
00:16:18,680 --> 00:16:21,001
[SPEAKER_01]: The wrong view, as the Buddha said, is the most blameworthy thing.

212
00:16:21,381 --> 00:16:23,961
[SPEAKER_01]: More blameworthy than killing, stealing, cheating and everything else.

213
00:16:25,002 --> 00:16:30,083
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the utmost, single, blameworthy thing in the entire universe, in the entire saṃsara.

214
00:16:31,344 --> 00:16:32,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking to know that which you don't.

215
00:16:34,585 --> 00:16:37,926
[SPEAKER_01]: Holding on to a view without questioning it, without understanding it.

216
00:16:46,140 --> 00:16:49,602
[SPEAKER_00]: It goes to... becomes a bit different, this.

217
00:16:51,643 --> 00:16:59,968
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's talk about what this person should be thinking, in terms of that metta, peaceful mind.

218
00:17:01,128 --> 00:17:05,511
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, let him think in safety and in happiness.

219
00:17:05,731 --> 00:17:08,593
[SPEAKER_00]: May creatures all be of a happy mind.

220
00:17:10,374 --> 00:17:19,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever breathing beings there may be, no matter what they are, eh... may creatures all be of a happy mind.

221
00:17:19,603 --> 00:17:26,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Let no one work for another's undoing, or even slight him at all, anywhere.

222
00:17:31,223 --> 00:17:52,579
[SPEAKER_01]: So, on the level of your thoughts, do not entertain even slightest, like, negative animosity, ill will, and so on.

223
00:17:52,699 --> 00:17:53,340
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

224
00:17:54,020 --> 00:17:55,341
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter what happens to you.

225
00:17:56,302 --> 00:17:57,323
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter on account of

226
00:17:58,842 --> 00:18:06,968
[SPEAKER_01]: What things happen to you, doesn't matter from whom, from what type of being, don't have a limited mind towards them.

227
00:18:08,309 --> 00:18:15,494
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you... because most people would take this and say, okay, this is the mantra that I must now say a million times.

228
00:18:15,554 --> 00:18:16,515
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll say it a million times.

229
00:18:17,316 --> 00:18:21,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Insection, bliss, micro-creatures, and on and on and on and on and on and on.

230
00:18:21,359 --> 00:18:24,781
[SPEAKER_00]: And you do that, do that, do that, do that.

231
00:18:25,182 --> 00:18:26,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you ask yourself,

232
00:18:27,212 --> 00:18:34,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Does repeating a mantra cultivate, maintain the understanding of the point of that mantra?

233
00:18:34,958 --> 00:18:36,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is it actually quite the opposite?

234
00:18:37,119 --> 00:18:40,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Focusing on repeating the mantra means ignoring the fact that it needs to be understood.

235
00:18:43,864 --> 00:18:44,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you...

236
00:18:45,105 --> 00:18:47,007
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you had to...

237
00:18:47,027 --> 00:18:51,010
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember doing a similar thing for a longer...

238
00:18:52,204 --> 00:18:55,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Repeating certain phrases.

239
00:18:55,886 --> 00:18:57,347
[SPEAKER_00]: May my mother be well.

240
00:18:57,407 --> 00:18:58,608
[SPEAKER_00]: May my sister this.

241
00:18:58,808 --> 00:19:00,609
[SPEAKER_01]: May I be this, that, that.

242
00:19:01,590 --> 00:19:02,451
[SPEAKER_00]: And you generate.

243
00:19:03,511 --> 00:19:05,553
[SPEAKER_00]: It's quite a pleasant thought.

244
00:19:05,913 --> 00:19:06,993
[SPEAKER_00]: And you have pleasant feelings.

245
00:19:08,114 --> 00:19:08,895
[SPEAKER_00]: And you feel good.

246
00:19:08,915 --> 00:19:09,715
[SPEAKER_00]: You just feel.

247
00:19:10,616 --> 00:19:10,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

248
00:19:12,017 --> 00:19:13,157
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not what it says there.

249
00:19:13,297 --> 00:19:15,159
[SPEAKER_01]: It says you should cultivate that mind.

250
00:19:16,019 --> 00:19:20,302
[SPEAKER_01]: The state of mind that has no animosity towards anything, breathing of any kind.

251
00:19:21,540 --> 00:19:25,505
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't cultivate state of mind by repeating the random words.

252
00:19:25,545 --> 00:19:27,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if that would be the case, the Buddha would have said so.

253
00:19:27,607 --> 00:19:29,249
[SPEAKER_01]: Just chant this and you'll be purified.

254
00:19:29,930 --> 00:19:36,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, whenever he talked about it, he talked about it in a dismissive manner of the other Brahmanic traditions they were doing.

255
00:19:37,659 --> 00:19:41,083
[SPEAKER_01]: And he would criticize them for chanting Vedas without understanding what they're chanting.

256
00:19:42,043 --> 00:19:48,205
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they're just parroting, basically, the verses because they developed the belief that the verses in themselves carry magic of purification.

257
00:19:48,966 --> 00:19:57,209
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, he said that even the original Brahmins that sort of compiled those Vedas and the wisdom in them, they actually understood them.

258
00:19:57,269 --> 00:19:58,349
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they were worthy men.

259
00:20:00,270 --> 00:20:02,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Brahmins of the old day, they understood the verses.

260
00:20:02,991 --> 00:20:04,091
[SPEAKER_01]: They understood the meaning.

261
00:20:04,111 --> 00:20:05,372
[SPEAKER_01]: They were not just chanting them.

262
00:20:06,212 --> 00:20:11,514
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you chant for the sake of remembering it, for the sake of then reflecting on it, to understand it, of course,

263
00:20:12,688 --> 00:20:19,091
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you chant because you believe that chanting purifies you and maintains your mind of non-ill-will, that's an utterly, completely wrong view.

264
00:20:20,172 --> 00:20:26,635
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, these people say this, I will say that as well.

265
00:20:26,815 --> 00:20:27,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

266
00:20:28,396 --> 00:20:29,797
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how I will partake in that.

267
00:20:29,857 --> 00:20:30,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I'm safe.

268
00:20:31,778 --> 00:20:32,178
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm in it.

269
00:20:33,239 --> 00:20:38,682
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I will just go by the hearsay, tradition and repeating words of others.

270
00:20:39,688 --> 00:20:43,250
[SPEAKER_01]: By mere repeating words of others, I will purify myself.

271
00:20:43,350 --> 00:20:48,092
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically practically what that view is, for those who believe that just chanting will purify you.

272
00:20:51,153 --> 00:20:51,973
[SPEAKER_00]: But let him think.

273
00:20:52,294 --> 00:20:53,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Let him think about this.

274
00:20:54,134 --> 00:20:55,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Let him reflect upon it.

275
00:20:55,155 --> 00:20:56,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Let him reflect.

276
00:20:56,836 --> 00:21:05,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Let him develop the state of mind that is limitless, that includes all these things you can have animosity towards.

277
00:21:06,400 --> 00:21:08,861
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you develop a state of mind free from animosity.

278
00:21:09,827 --> 00:21:13,249
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all the things within that, that you could hate, you cannot hate.

279
00:21:14,490 --> 00:21:17,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Because hate is rooted in the state of mind, not in things that you hate.

280
00:21:20,994 --> 00:21:23,756
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can also ask... yeah, you think about it.

281
00:21:24,216 --> 00:21:27,638
[SPEAKER_00]: In safety and in happiness, may creatures all be... Yeah.

282
00:21:27,678 --> 00:21:31,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Think about those verses, about guidelines for your contemplation.

283
00:21:31,661 --> 00:21:32,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

284
00:21:32,141 --> 00:21:32,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

285
00:21:32,481 --> 00:21:33,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I...?

286
00:21:33,081 --> 00:21:33,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

287
00:21:33,262 --> 00:21:35,223
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha said nothing that breaches... Why is that?

288
00:21:35,403 --> 00:21:35,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

289
00:21:36,694 --> 00:21:42,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if you can't figure out why, take the Buddha's word and trust, then figure out how to achieve that.

290
00:21:43,279 --> 00:21:52,566
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how will I have one state of mind that will not hate this wide array of diverse beings and objects?

291
00:21:54,627 --> 00:21:59,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that achievable by chasing down the diversity of the objects?

292
00:22:00,678 --> 00:22:06,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is it achievable by maintaining the uniformity of one non-hateful mind?

293
00:22:07,620 --> 00:22:08,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is more likely.

294
00:22:10,020 --> 00:22:15,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it about cultivating the pleasure of a particular thought?

295
00:22:17,642 --> 00:22:21,583
[SPEAKER_00]: And the avoidance of the displeasure of... Another particular thought.

296
00:22:21,603 --> 00:22:22,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Another thought.

297
00:22:22,843 --> 00:22:29,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is it cultivating a thought... That is above either pleasure...

298
00:22:30,317 --> 00:22:31,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Or displeasure.

299
00:22:31,038 --> 00:22:31,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

300
00:22:32,259 --> 00:22:33,160
[SPEAKER_00]: The answer's obviously there.

301
00:22:33,320 --> 00:22:34,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

302
00:22:38,524 --> 00:22:39,125
[SPEAKER_00]: It continues.

303
00:22:39,785 --> 00:22:52,257
[SPEAKER_00]: And just as a mother, with her life, protects her only child, so let him then, for every living thing, protect his boundless mind.

304
00:22:52,277 --> 00:22:53,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

305
00:22:53,538 --> 00:22:55,079
[SPEAKER_00]: We spoke about it before.

306
00:22:55,259 --> 00:22:55,900
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite obvious.

307
00:22:57,158 --> 00:23:05,202
[SPEAKER_01]: You have contemporary translations that skew that, and they basically imply that, as the mother protects the child, you protect all these living beings.

308
00:23:05,402 --> 00:23:09,104
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't say, so let him protect every living being.

309
00:23:09,244 --> 00:23:11,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Like his own child.

310
00:23:11,045 --> 00:23:15,848
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, let him protect his one mind, like the mother protects one child, one son, literally.

311
00:23:16,968 --> 00:23:21,071
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your only son, that's your mind.

312
00:23:21,491 --> 00:23:22,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mind is your only child.

313
00:23:23,672 --> 00:23:24,813
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will not forget

314
00:23:25,717 --> 00:23:29,478
[SPEAKER_01]: About cultivation, protection and maintenance of that child.

315
00:23:29,978 --> 00:23:32,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you do, it could die.

316
00:23:33,699 --> 00:23:36,219
[SPEAKER_01]: And it could result in your own death.

317
00:23:36,259 --> 00:23:42,081
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you protect your mind as your own child, you will automatically protect all living beings.

318
00:23:42,861 --> 00:23:53,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when there is action of non-protection of all living beings, it's not rooted in those beings or non those beings, it's rooted in your mind of your will.

319
00:23:54,308 --> 00:24:01,389
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you protect your mind from ill-will, no act of ill-will can be exercised towards anything in the world.

320
00:24:01,930 --> 00:24:04,890
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by proxy, then, you protect the entire world.

321
00:24:05,430 --> 00:24:11,012
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how the Buddha explained arahant, by practicing for his own benefit, practicing for the benefit of the whole universe.

322
00:24:14,372 --> 00:24:16,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but we want to... yeah, people want to see... Well, no.

323
00:24:16,653 --> 00:24:20,514
[SPEAKER_01]: For most people, that internal transparent dimension is not transparent.

324
00:24:21,214 --> 00:24:22,454
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... they don't see it.

325
00:24:23,319 --> 00:24:30,562
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they measure goodness or non-goodness by the level of engagement with others and with the world.

326
00:24:31,122 --> 00:24:37,385
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from that point of view, of an ignorant, putruginous view, Arahant is the most selfish person in the world.

327
00:24:37,645 --> 00:24:38,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Swayed by the emotions.

328
00:24:39,606 --> 00:24:39,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

329
00:24:39,906 --> 00:24:41,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Swayed by the emotions of others.

330
00:24:41,247 --> 00:24:42,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

331
00:24:43,068 --> 00:24:48,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Using the measure of emotions for whether things are good or bad.

332
00:24:48,370 --> 00:24:50,231
[SPEAKER_01]: This makes us feel good, thus it's good.

333
00:24:50,491 --> 00:24:50,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

334
00:24:52,390 --> 00:24:57,714
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you see, ironically then, many people approach the dhamma practice rooted in that.

335
00:24:58,754 --> 00:25:03,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'll practice freedom from my dependence on feelings by following what makes me feel good.

336
00:25:04,638 --> 00:25:05,859
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a contradiction in terms.

337
00:25:11,022 --> 00:25:13,844
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what we said that in the previous... one of the previous talks.

338
00:25:15,185 --> 00:25:17,247
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't remember what order we shared them, but...

339
00:25:18,979 --> 00:25:25,628
[SPEAKER_01]: If you just... you don't need to practice kindness and non-ill will towards the world or towards anyone in particular.

340
00:25:26,069 --> 00:25:29,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Just practice it towards whatever present feeling is enduring in your experience.

341
00:25:29,614 --> 00:25:31,316
[SPEAKER_01]: And you've covered the entire world?

342
00:25:33,459 --> 00:25:34,160
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything to breathe.

343
00:25:36,009 --> 00:25:40,610
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot hate, because you stop resisting the discomfort of a feeling that's presently enduring.

344
00:25:40,650 --> 00:25:44,111
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you truly want to help... That's the only way you can help.

345
00:25:44,171 --> 00:25:45,171
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize, yeah.

346
00:25:45,451 --> 00:25:59,054
[SPEAKER_01]: You can have a desire to help, but you can choose to ignore understanding what is truly helpful on account of that desire, or you can choose to not ignore and make the effort to understand what is truly helpful.

347
00:25:59,934 --> 00:26:02,155
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is truly helpful is the mind of non-ill will.

348
00:26:08,467 --> 00:26:12,329
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you can do that, then you can potentially teach others to do that.

349
00:26:12,369 --> 00:26:12,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

350
00:26:15,971 --> 00:26:16,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Which protects them.

351
00:26:17,452 --> 00:26:17,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

352
00:26:17,793 --> 00:26:21,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can build people houses, food, give them food.

353
00:26:21,995 --> 00:26:25,837
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all mundane, mundane, pertaining to the world.

354
00:26:25,937 --> 00:26:33,262
[SPEAKER_01]: You can do that, if that's your goal, but you can't expect to do that and practice dhamma at the same time, because those goals are mutually exclusive.

355
00:26:34,512 --> 00:26:41,634
[SPEAKER_01]: If you can help others and not compromise the lifestyle that's dedicated to practice in the dhamma that you understood, that's fine, of course.

356
00:26:42,134 --> 00:26:43,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody's drowning, you pull them out.

357
00:26:43,694 --> 00:26:47,615
[SPEAKER_01]: You're like, oh, well, I will teach them dhamma in these three seconds before they drown.

358
00:26:49,035 --> 00:27:01,798
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you dedicate your life to saving people from drowning, in those particular instances, and on account of that neglect your own mind, your own child of non-ill-will, well, that doesn't help anyone.

359
00:27:03,325 --> 00:27:06,327
[SPEAKER_01]: The help is very limited, very fleeting.

360
00:27:07,167 --> 00:27:07,347
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

361
00:27:07,928 --> 00:27:08,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Not substantial.

362
00:27:11,970 --> 00:27:25,338
[SPEAKER_00]: So, above, below and all around, untroubled, with no enemy, and while he stands, walks, sits or lies down, free from drowsiness,

363
00:27:26,500 --> 00:27:28,601
[SPEAKER_00]: Let him resolve upon this mindfulness.

364
00:27:28,921 --> 00:27:29,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

365
00:27:30,101 --> 00:27:31,502
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the peaceful life.

366
00:27:31,722 --> 00:27:31,942
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

367
00:27:32,402 --> 00:27:37,164
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot resolve upon things that are not clear.

368
00:27:40,606 --> 00:27:46,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Like there's instances in the suttas of a person who becomes arahant by resolving upon wisdom.

369
00:27:49,309 --> 00:27:53,631
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he resolves upon that which he gained insight with wisdom.

370
00:27:55,795 --> 00:27:58,778
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in order to resolve upon something, you need to know what that is.

371
00:28:00,459 --> 00:28:01,560
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to be clear about it.

372
00:28:01,580 --> 00:28:02,801
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to be familiar with it.

373
00:28:02,961 --> 00:28:09,808
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to have made the effort to remove any opaqueness, any ambiguity in regard to the meaning and nature of it.

374
00:28:10,488 --> 00:28:14,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Once it's sufficiently clear, that's when that resolve can occur.

375
00:28:14,492 --> 00:28:16,273
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when you can maintain that resolve.

376
00:28:16,353 --> 00:28:17,975
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it can result in arahantyam.

377
00:28:19,937 --> 00:28:28,683
[SPEAKER_01]: It's free from drowsiness, but free from... Free from sloth, free from lack of clarity, free from laziness in regard to maintaining the clarity.

378
00:28:33,646 --> 00:28:43,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Not somehow energetic, like magical energy, or free from... it's not tied.

379
00:28:44,909 --> 00:28:49,491
[SPEAKER_00]: He's got this abundant energy flowing through his body of love.

380
00:28:49,631 --> 00:28:49,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

381
00:28:49,971 --> 00:28:50,731
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's not.

382
00:28:50,831 --> 00:28:51,892
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

383
00:28:52,452 --> 00:28:53,993
[SPEAKER_01]: It refers to the clarity of the meaning.

384
00:28:54,353 --> 00:28:54,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

385
00:28:54,893 --> 00:28:56,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why you can resolve upon it with mindfulness.

386
00:28:58,435 --> 00:28:59,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Not this energy of love.

387
00:28:59,715 --> 00:29:08,619
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be tired, but tiredness does not induce losing the sight of the right context.

388
00:29:09,399 --> 00:29:12,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Does not induce forgetting about your only child.

389
00:29:12,901 --> 00:29:13,421
[SPEAKER_01]: You're tired.

390
00:29:14,494 --> 00:29:16,075
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're still clear about what's important.

391
00:29:17,216 --> 00:29:19,397
[SPEAKER_01]: You're still clear about what you must protect.

392
00:29:20,838 --> 00:29:21,598
[SPEAKER_01]: You still protect it.

393
00:29:22,378 --> 00:29:24,119
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when you're not tired, you protect it even more.

394
00:29:24,860 --> 00:29:25,560
[SPEAKER_01]: You cultivate it.

395
00:29:29,883 --> 00:29:33,124
[SPEAKER_00]: The last verse is...

396
00:29:33,505 --> 00:29:34,145
[SPEAKER_00]: When he has...

397
00:29:36,346 --> 00:29:39,168
[SPEAKER_00]: When he's not holding to any views...

398
00:29:40,603 --> 00:29:47,147
[SPEAKER_00]: He's virtuous and has perfected seeing and purges greed from... for sensual desires.

399
00:29:48,267 --> 00:29:48,708
[SPEAKER_00]: He surely...

400
00:29:48,728 --> 00:29:49,968
[SPEAKER_01]: But not holding to wrong views.

401
00:29:50,088 --> 00:29:51,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, not holding to wrong views.

402
00:29:51,569 --> 00:29:54,531
[SPEAKER_00]: Virtuous, perfecting knowledge and seeing.

403
00:29:56,132 --> 00:29:57,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Free from sensual desires.

404
00:29:57,773 --> 00:29:59,794
[SPEAKER_00]: He no longer gets born again.

405
00:29:59,814 --> 00:30:02,175
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when arahant becomes... he goes beyond the views.

406
00:30:02,555 --> 00:30:03,216
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it means.

407
00:30:03,536 --> 00:30:03,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

408
00:30:04,453 --> 00:30:08,376
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need the right view in order to overcome the wrong views.

409
00:30:08,956 --> 00:30:12,578
[SPEAKER_01]: But for an arahant, basically, he overcomes even the right view.

410
00:30:16,081 --> 00:30:28,569
[SPEAKER_01]: That's that sutta, it has the Noble Eightfold Path, and for an arahant, it's like a tenfold path, whereby he overcomes even that dependence on the view, the right view, the right criteria, he even extinguishes that.

411
00:30:29,809 --> 00:30:33,392
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he resolves upon clarity... With the right view?

412
00:30:33,572 --> 00:30:33,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

413
00:30:34,859 --> 00:30:38,983
[SPEAKER_01]: You maintain it, you cultivate it until it ripens and then even that falls off.

414
00:30:39,183 --> 00:30:44,508
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, once you use the raft that you built, you cross the shore and then you leave the raft.

415
00:30:44,989 --> 00:30:46,370
[SPEAKER_01]: And when do you leave the raft?

416
00:30:46,450 --> 00:30:48,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you know when you're safe on the other side of the shore.

417
00:30:49,625 --> 00:30:51,027
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like halfway through pedaling.

418
00:30:52,008 --> 00:30:53,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, do I abandon raft now?

419
00:30:53,630 --> 00:30:56,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Or... You cannot doubt it, because it's not up to you anymore.

420
00:30:56,934 --> 00:30:57,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

421
00:30:57,735 --> 00:31:00,438
[SPEAKER_00]: They say that you abandon this raft and go to the other shore.

422
00:31:00,638 --> 00:31:01,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, I'll abandon now.

423
00:31:02,400 --> 00:31:03,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right, right.

424
00:31:03,662 --> 00:31:04,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

425
00:31:04,102 --> 00:31:06,845
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll preemptively abandon it, because you shouldn't hold on to anything.

426
00:31:06,885 --> 00:31:07,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

427
00:31:08,018 --> 00:31:09,539
[SPEAKER_01]: No, then that's often misunderstood.

428
00:31:09,559 --> 00:31:19,265
[SPEAKER_01]: I know in the same, like the contemporary chanting of that, like, not hold to any views, or they even now, I think, since in some translations, don't hold to fixed views.

429
00:31:20,005 --> 00:31:21,466
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, well, that's a fixed view.

430
00:31:22,227 --> 00:31:24,908
[SPEAKER_01]: I shall not hold to fixed views is your fixed view.

431
00:31:26,029 --> 00:31:35,695
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is no way out of that, except finding the right type of fixed view, which is the fixed view of Sotāpanna, that he developed from understanding the Buddha's teaching.

432
00:31:36,717 --> 00:31:44,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, using that fixed view to overcome all the kind of underlying tendencies and kilesas and so on, then that fixed view will wither away as well.

433
00:31:46,281 --> 00:31:47,442
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when you're an arahant.

434
00:31:49,083 --> 00:31:59,508
[SPEAKER_01]: But people think, oh, if I abstain from declaring my view, I somehow skate between the views and I will be free from the views by not holding on to views, which is the view that I hold.

435
00:32:02,349 --> 00:32:04,330
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that last verse refers to the arahantship.

436
00:32:06,923 --> 00:32:16,987
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only one truly who can say, I don't hold to any views anymore, is the one whose right view has withered away, extinguished itself through the right effort.

437
00:32:20,008 --> 00:32:21,028
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he went beyond the views.

438
00:32:30,091 --> 00:32:31,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's the full development of...

439
00:32:32,390 --> 00:32:33,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's not a choice.

440
00:32:34,391 --> 00:32:38,473
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not so that when he makes a choice, I will not hold to my right view and he becomes an arahant.

441
00:32:39,133 --> 00:32:40,754
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a result of the right practice.

442
00:32:41,634 --> 00:32:49,458
[SPEAKER_01]: That the Buddha outlines there and then says if you practice like that, you will not anymore be attached to any view, even the right view.

443
00:32:50,018 --> 00:32:51,098
[SPEAKER_01]: You will be an arahant then.

444
00:32:52,799 --> 00:32:54,260
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the right view that brought him there.

445
00:32:54,940 --> 00:32:56,941
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the raft that took him to the other shore.

